# Module 1.5: Two Fundamental Tasks

## CONTEXT: The Two Questions Every Supervised Model Answers

Imagine you are the head of data science at BookMyShow. Two product managers walk into your office the same morning with different requests.

PM 1 says: "I need to know exactly how many tickets a new Bollywood release will sell on opening weekend. Give me the number. Our vendor contracts depend on it."

PM 2 says: "I need to know whether a user who just landed on our homepage will buy a ticket today or leave without purchasing. Yes or no. That determines whether we show them a discount popup."

Both problems use supervised learning. Both have labeled historical data. But they need completely different approaches, different algorithms, different ways to measure success, and different ways to think about the answer.

PM 1 needs a number on a continuous scale. That is regression.
PM 2 needs a category from a fixed set. That is classification.

This module goes deep on both. Not just what they are, but how they actually work, what algorithms to reach for, and crucially, how you know if your model is actually good.

---

## CONCEPT: Regression in Depth

### What Regression Really Does

At its core, regression finds the mathematical relationship between your input features and a continuous output. It is trying to fit a line (or curve) through your data points so that for any new input, you can read off the predicted output from that line.

Think of it as finding the best-fit trend in your data.

### The BookMyShow Ticket Sales Example

Your labeled training data looks like this:

```
movie_rating | screens | lead_actor_popularity | opening_weekend_tickets_sold
8.2          | 3200    | 92                    | 4,200,000
6.8          | 1800    | 71                    | 1,100,000
7.5          | 2600    | 85                    | 2,800,000
9.1          | 4100    | 98                    | 7,600,000
5.9          | 900     | 45                    | 380,000
```

The regression model learns: "Every additional 0.1 in movie rating tends to add about 300,000 tickets sold. Every additional screen adds about 1,500 tickets. Lead actor popularity above 80 creates a non-linear boost..."

Now when a new movie comes in with rating 7.8, 2,900 screens, and lead popularity 88, the model outputs: approximately 3,200,000 tickets.

That is regression. A specific, continuous number as the answer.

### How Do You Know If Your Regression Model Is Good?

This is where evaluation metrics come in. You need a way to measure how wrong your predictions are.

Here is an interactive feel for the two most important regression metrics:The key insight from that interactive: MAE (Mean Absolute Error) treats all errors equally. RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error) squares the errors first, which means one very wrong prediction hurts your score far more than five slightly wrong predictions. Use RMSE when large errors are especially costly (predicting patient wait times, stock prices). Use MAE when all errors are equally bad (predicting delivery times).

