## CONCEPT: The Overfitting Trap

Before we go to the lab, there is one more concept every student must understand. It is the most common mistake beginners make.

### The Exam Cramming Analogy

Imagine a student preparing for a Data Structures exam. Instead of understanding concepts, they memorize every question from the previous five years of past papers, word for word.

On exam day, if the professor reuses old questions, this student scores 100%. But if the professor asks slightly different questions (which professors always do), the student fails completely. They memorized the patterns in past papers, but they did not actually learn the underlying concepts.

This is overfitting. Your model memorizes the training data so precisely that it loses the ability to generalize to new, unseen data.

The solution is measuring your model on data it has never seen during training. That held-out set is called the test set.

---The overfitting model scores 99% on training data. Put it on new data and it collapses. The good fit model scores 85% on both. That consistent performance across train and test is the real sign of a model that has genuinely learned.

